A||N||9||gkozlov||Quarter window Replacements||||1||
Z||000000||gkozlov||07-11-2000||03:56 PM||||I have a 86 SE w/plastic qtr windows. I have the glass ones to replce them. Can I do this?<BR>If yes how and if no why not?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Jumpwing_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||286SE'S||07-11-2000||05:32 PM||||I saw an 86 SE at the junk yard and it had a <BR>really clean drivers sail panel. When I got it off I found that it was a GLASS panel! So <BR>they do fit. The difference is that the glass ones only have a bolt below the Pontiac emblem and on the plastic ones the bolt goes to a clip that holds the plastic frame. The plastic sails can be pried off but the glass ones will shatter if you don't take out the bolt. You must remove the interior plastic trim and remove the bolt that holds them in. Since you have a glass panel you know exactly where it it. Go for it. The glass ones will look great for a long time.<BR>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000002||gkozlov||07-11-2000||05:35 PM||||Im sorry I didnt explain. I only have the glass qtr window inserts. Not the entire qtr windows.<P><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Jumpwing_2.gif">  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by gkozlov (edited 07-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||spike||07-11-2000||07:44 PM||||I will be changing my Quater's also in the near future. Does anyone know how to bend plexiglass. I think it would not be all that difficult to make your own. The only real problem that I can see is that the plexiglass would have to be bent to met-up with the door opening. Anyone have any ideals. Could you heat it some way. I refuse to pay 200.00 U.S. for a pair.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||07-11-2000||09:45 PM||||Spike<BR>Only the fastback panels are about $100 ea the coupe's are much much cheaper about $25 I think||||reg||1
Z||000005||Old Lar||07-11-2000||10:39 PM||||Spike, the GT windows are curved front to back and top to bottom, plus they have the lip to fit the door molding.  If you feel they are that easy to make go to.  There will be a ready market for them.  GM invested 1/2 million in the molding machines.  So maybe you can do as good a job with a hair dryer flexing the material, which you can get for free somewhere.||||reg||1
Z||000006||TOM||07-12-2000||12:41 AM||||Why such an a$$ old lar. They are not that hard to make. I have made them. But I didnt have the artwork applied. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||07-12-2000||02:27 AM||||The notchback 1/4 windows can be swapped between all years but you need the original frames. The glass window frames have a rubber seal that holds the glass in place. The plastic ones are taped in. You could try taping the glass ones in but due to weight and vibration it may fall off the car. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||2
Z||000008||gkozlov||07-12-2000||05:53 AM||||Thanks Oreif. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||spike||07-12-2000||08:48 AM||||I do have a Fastback Fiero. I am not saying that my home made quarters will look just like the factory ones which GM produced. I have always enjoyed having something different about my car which will make it stand out from the rest. This is the reason I love Fieros so much. They seem to be scarce in Montreal. Thanks Tom. I have built a Sub box to hold 2 10 inch subs before when everyone said it can't be done. Anything is possible if you have the passion.||||reg||1
